---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PersonSchema from '../components/PersonSchema.astro';
import PageShell from '../components/PageShell.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import JourneyMovement from '../components/JourneyMovement.astro';
import ArcDiagram from '../components/ArcDiagram.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const certifications = [
  'Certified Kubernetes Administrator',
  'AWS Certified Security – Specialty',
  'AWS Certified Solutions Architect – Associate',
  'AWS Certified SysOps Administrator – Associate',
  'Google Cloud Professional Cloud Architect',
  'Google Cloud Associate Cloud Engineer',
  'CCNA',
  'HCNA',
];

const education = [
  { degree: 'Master of Engineering, Telecommunications', school: 'UNSW Sydney', years: '2018 – 2019' },
  { degree: 'B.Tech, Electronics & Communications Engineering', school: 'Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Coimbatore', years: '2013 – 2017' },
];

---
<BaseLayout
  title="About · Vikranth Subramanian"
  description="A decade of work across signals, networks, infrastructure, platforms, and products."
>
  <PersonSchema slot="head" />

  <PageShell>
    <PageHeader title="About">
      <p class="lede-lg">
        I've never really changed fields. I've changed altitude.
      </p>
      <p class="lede opening-body">
        For about a decade I've been circling the same question from higher and higher
        up: how does a complex system actually behave, and how do you build something
        that holds up inside it? It started with radar returns and antennas. It's
        currently products. The question hasn't changed much.
      </p>
    </PageHeader>

    <ArcDiagram />

    <div class="journey">
      <JourneyMovement
        index={1}
        title="Signals"
        system="Radar returns, antennas, noise"
        period="2013 – 2017"
        affiliation="B.Tech ECE, Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham · Ananth Technologies"
      >
        <p>
          I started underneath the software, at the layer where a signal is still
          physics. I spent my undergraduate years on the problem of extracting truth
          from noise: tracking targets that don't want to be tracked, detecting returns
          buried in clutter.
        </p>
        <p>
          That work turned into three publications: non-linear tracking of a submarine
          using an Extended Kalman Filter, a fuzzy-logic approach to target detection in
          MIMO radar, and a metaheuristic method for finding and tracking targets. At
          Ananth Technologies I worked on antenna design and RF systems, and simulated
          antenna radiation patterns.
        </p>
        <p>
          I didn't know it then, but I was learning the thing I'd keep doing: a system is
          always telling you what it's doing. Most of the work is building the instrument
          that can hear it.
        </p>
      </JourneyMovement>

      <JourneyMovement
        index={2}
        title="Networks"
        system="Enterprise network behaviour"
        period="2018 – 2019"
        affiliation="MEng Telecommunications, UNSW Sydney · RCITI"
      >
        <p>
          I moved to Australia and moved up a layer. Same question, new system: instead
          of radar returns, enterprise networks.
        </p>
        <p>
          My thesis was <em>Scalable and Real-Time Monitoring Network Behaviour of
          Enterprise Hosts</em>, which meant detecting malicious activity inside a
          network by watching how its hosts actually behave, in real time, at a scale
          where you can't inspect everything. Packet inspection, traffic analysis and
          performance characterisation with Wireshark, TCPdump and NFF-go; SDN work with
          the Ryu controller and Mininet for emulation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Alongside it I was a research assistant at RCITI, applying machine learning to
          improve recovery times in emergency periods, and a UNSW HERO Ambassador. That
          last role was my first real taste of the other half of building things:
          convincing people, working with industry partners, and turning an idea into
          something an organisation would actually adopt.
        </p>
      </JourneyMovement>

      <JourneyMovement
        index={3}
        title="Infrastructure"
        system="Cloud estates at migration scale"
        period="2018 – 2020"
        affiliation="The Migration Company · Sourced Group"
      >
        <p>
          Then the system got bigger and less theoretical. At The Migration Company and
          Sourced Group I worked on cloud migrations at genuine scale, the kind where
          you're moving hundreds of terabytes and millions of users, and the interesting
          problems are never the ones in the architecture diagram.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is where I stopped thinking about systems as things you observe and
          started thinking about them as things you're responsible for. Different
          relationship entirely.
        </p>
      </JourneyMovement>

      <JourneyMovement
        index={4}
        title="Zero to one"
        system="The company itself"
        period="2020 – 2022"
        affiliation="Kivera · Founding Engineer"
      >
        <p>
          I joined Kivera as the first engineer, which meant I did a bit of everything a
          startup needs and learned that "the system" now included the company. Product
          decisions, infrastructure, security, whatever the week demanded.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's the period that changed how I work most. When there's nobody to hand a
          problem to, you stop waiting for a spec.
        </p>
      </JourneyMovement>

      <JourneyMovement
        index={5}
        title="Platforms under load"
        system="Production systems and the teams on them"
        period="2022 – 2024"
        affiliation="Lendi Group · Beforepay Group"
      >
        <p>
          Back to scale, but with the founding-engineer instinct intact. At Lendi I moved
          from DevOps into senior platform engineering over two years; at Beforepay I
          worked on platform in financial services, where the cost of getting it wrong is
          measured in someone's actual money.
        </p>
        <p>
          Platform work taught me the part of systems thinking the technical layers
          don't: a platform's real users are engineers, and a platform that's correct but
          unpleasant to use fails just as completely as one that's broken.
        </p>
      </JourneyMovement>

      <JourneyMovement
        index={6}
        title="Products"
        system="What should be built, not just how"
        period="2024 – 2026"
        affiliation="Nullify · Lead Product Engineer"
      >
        {/* The `{' '}` below is load-bearing. compressHTML (Astro's default) deletes
            whitespace that sits against a node boundary rather than collapsing it, so
            the source newline after </em> vanished and this shipped as "build thisand
            became". Whitespace inside an uninterrupted text run is only collapsed, which
            is why the other line breaks here need no help. Keep this comment outside the
            <p>: placing it between two text lines splits the run and reintroduces the
            very bug it documents. */}
        <p>
          At Nullify I worked at the intersection of AI, security and user experience,
          and the job changed shape. It stopped being only <em>how do we build this</em>{' '}
          and became <em>what should this be</em>. That's the altitude I'd been climbing
          toward without quite naming it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The problems were the genuinely unsolved ones. How does an agent remember, and
          what should it retrieve when it does? What context belongs in front of a model,
          and what quietly poisons the output? Where does an agent actually fit inside a
          workflow that already exists, as opposed to a workflow rebuilt around it? These
          questions do not have settled answers yet, which is exactly why they were worth
          working on.
        </p>
        <p>
          Alongside that, securing the codebases themselves. I designed and built the
          triage system from zero to one, and worked across every edge of the product
          rather than owning one surface, because robustness lives in the seams between
          features far more often than inside them.
        </p>
      </JourneyMovement>
    </div>

    <section class="next" aria-labelledby="next-heading">
      <p class="next-eyebrow">The seventh movement</p>
      <h2 id="next-heading">Ownership</h2>

      <p class="next-lede">
        Every step of this has been the same move: getting closer to deciding what gets
        built. Signals, networks, infrastructure, platforms, products. The next rung is
        ownership. Building something from zero where I'm accountable for whether it's
        the right thing, not only whether it works.
      </p>

      <div class="next-grid">
        <div class="next-col">
          <h3>What I'm drawn to</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Problems nobody has settled answers for yet</li>
            <li>Zero to one, where the question is still what to build</li>
            <li>AI systems that have to survive contact with real users</li>
            <li>The infrastructure and security underneath them</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="next-col">
          <h3>What I bring</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A decade across the whole stack, from signal physics to product</li>
            <li>Founding-engineer experience, twice over</li>
            <li>Comfort with ambiguity and a bias for shipping</li>
            <li>The instinct to ask what a system is telling you first</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="next-cta">
        <p>
          If you're building something where that instinct is useful, I'd like to hear
          about it.
        </p>
        <p class="contact">
          <a class="contact-primary" href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
          <span class="contact-links">
            <a href={SITE.social.linkedin} rel="me noopener">LinkedIn</a>
            <a href={SITE.social.github} rel="me noopener">GitHub</a>
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="details">
      <section aria-labelledby="education-heading">
        <h2 id="education-heading">Education</h2>
        <ul class="plain">
          {education.map((e) => (
            <li>
              <strong>{e.degree}</strong>
              <span>{e.school}</span>
              <span class="dim">{e.years}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="certs-heading">
        <h2 id="certs-heading">Certifications</h2>
        <ul class="plain">{certifications.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}</ul>
      </section>

    </div>
  </PageShell>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .opening-body { margin-top: var(--space-4); }
  .journey { margin-bottom: var(--space-16); }
  /* The close is the page's destination, not a footnote, so it gets the only
     accent-lit surface on the page and the widest measure. */
  .next {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding: var(--space-12) var(--space-8);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background:
      radial-gradient(
        120% 140% at 100% 0%,
        color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 12%, transparent),
        transparent 60%
      ),
      var(--color-surface);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }
  /* A gradient hairline across the top edge, echoing the hero accent. */
  .next::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0 0 auto 0;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--gradient-accent);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .next-eyebrow {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-accent-quiet);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }
  .next h2 { font-size: var(--text-3xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .next-lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: var(--color-text);
    max-width: var(--width-prose);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
  }

  .next-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(260px, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-8);
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .next-col h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-dim);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .next-col ul { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .next-col li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
  .next-col li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.6em;
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .next-cta { margin-top: var(--space-8); }
  .next-cta p { color: var(--color-text); max-width: var(--width-prose); }
  .contact {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .contact-primary {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text);
    text-decoration-color: var(--color-accent);
    /* The address is one unbreakable token, and at 200% text zoom it is wider than
       a phone viewport. .next sets `overflow: hidden`, so without this the tail is
       cut off with no scrollbar to reach it and the contact address the page exists
       to hand over is unreadable. A mid-address break beats a truncated one. */
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* wrap so the two links stack rather than running past the panel edge at 200% zoom. */
  .contact-links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--text-sm); }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .next { padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-4); }
    .next h2 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); }
  }
  .details {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }
  /* Eyebrow-style label heading. Every other uppercase label in the system
     (PageHeader .eyebrow, PostCard time, ProjectCard .role, JourneyMovement
     .period, ExperienceTimeline .period, NowBlock, Hero, ResearchEntry .meta)
     is --text-xs; this was the lone --text-sm outlier. */
  .details h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-dim);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .plain { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  .plain li { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); }
  .plain strong { color: var(--color-text); font-weight: 600; }
  .dim { color: var(--color-text-dim); font-size: var(--text-xs); }
</style>
